hey this is danny O'Dwyer speaking the  wonderful words of mr. kevin van oort  this is gamespot's review and progress  of destiny there's a stark and uneasy  beauty to destiny it follows forth from  the methane pools that poison Venus's  landscapes and it billows from within  the fissures crisscrossing Earth's own  moon these environments are both  stunning and lifeless playing destiny is  to move and shoot through spaces that  don't feel entirely real they are both  tremendously gorgeous and eerily  synthetic like Destiny's mechanics the  atmosphere seems designed to avoid  alienating the pickiest of focus groups  and don't take that to mean that destiny  is a negative experience thus far this  is the type of game that you would use  the word polished to describe it was  clearly expensive to make with  meticulous mechanics and fluid controls  that you rarely have to fight against  there's a halo ish feel to some of those  mechanics but this game is never as  cartoonish as that other sci-fi shooter  so in the most mechanical respects  destiny feels absolutely fantastic  taking down a Hobgoblin or a wizard with  a few shots at your pulse rifle is a  powerful feeling and your foes are  worthy ones if rather bullets bungie the  context for these encounters is slow to  build tension destiny's dialogue is  dominated by the hovering robot called a  ghost that accompanies you so the  appearance of other characters is a  welcome one nevertheless Destiny's  dialogue comes from the diablo 3 school  of cliche offering up such gems as I've  got a bad feeling about this without  enough cheeky energy for you to believe  that this could actually be a star wars  reference destiny means for you to join  others in your quests you can play on  your own but there comes a time when  doing so means a bit of grinding areas  you've already visited fortunately if  bottles get difficult most combat spaces  are eloquently designed and give you  plenty of room to find cover should you  need time to charge up your shield it's  better to find others to join you which  is easy enough to do from your friends  list then the hub area known as the  tower or even in the world at large  where you stumble upon other players if  you played the alpha or beta test you  know more or less how destiny feels  however there's little to find fault  with in this regard as for the loot the  story in the world building while the  jury is still out for now however  destiny has the look and feel of a big  budget shooter and the structure of a  lean newt driven role-playing game a  combination of borderlands poppy  and destiny greatly refines at the same  time there is a sense that you're just  going through the motions and that the  excellent mechanics just need a chance  to break free we'll bring you a full  review of Destiny soon on gamespot com
 

